ould have done for those people uh in  that office well I think we did what we  could do for the people there  we took some administrative  responsibility just because there are  people we don't run the office we're not  the legal officer that can do the things  that need to be done although Mike is  deputized so he can function there you  know if there were anything maybe the  public administrator shouldn't even be  in elected office could we have done  something well I know one of our members  tried to do that when she was in the  legislature but and I think it takes the  legislative action to do that obviously  final question here we understand that  the obviously the prosecution is going  that's a whole separate deal but just at  the county offices right now what has  been going on with regards to the public  administrator's office to sort of the  machinations leading up to this what has  it been like at the County office since  this took place well we've  some of the people who were there are  not there any longer they've been  reassigned to other departments Mike  Murphy has taken responsibility for  basically everything in the office  there's an election going on right now  and eventually someone will be elected  to fill that post so we're going to  continue down the path of managing it  through Mike Murphy through the election  we'll make a determination as to  appointing after the election it has  nothing to do with who wins it has more  to do with what makes the most sense  uh let's switch gears let's talk about  the the election itself you're up for  re-election obviously  um and let's talk about what's happened  in the last couple of years with the  County Commission  um the biggest challenges you've had to  deal with through covid and everything  else how would you grade your  performance getting through the depths  of the pandemic  frankly I think we worked well together  there was always going to be conflict  both internally because we have  different views about things and  um with others you know the business  Community was frantic to get back open  early on no one was coming to Las Vegas  so because we're dependent upon tourism  and travelers and Airlines were shut  down and there were empty buildings it  it was something that was it didn't  require a lot of action on the part of  anyone but it was not we who were  shutting down or making determinations  about  what was essential that was a  gubernatorial determination what we did  was we worked within the confines of his  emergency powers to enable a restart at  some point and we were working very hard  trying to make the case where it was  appropriate that it was time to reopen  some things and to allow other things to  begin to get ready to be open that was  something that we did on the Fly because  there was no handbook for that we had  never operated under a circumstance ever  where we had a gubernatorial emergency  order that took all power  to the governor's office  we people who would say that we should  have done this or we should have done  that maybe there are some things we  learned that we'd do a little  differently but I think all in all we  did a really good job and you know I was  on the phone and with both state leaders  and with businesses we had issues  related to liability that were  especially important and top of mind  with many of these folks we had  landlords who had locked out tenants and  tenants had product and goods that they  couldn't even secure they were very  concerned about rent what happens to  their rent there's no provision in their  agreement that says reliably that they  don't have to pay rent right time but on  that note you're talking about you know  there's no real playbook for this and we  did what we could given what took place  from from Carson City  there are a lot of people including your  opponent who would who are blaming  anyone who was in office during the  depths of the pandemic for what has now  taken place in the struggles for  business to get back on track what do  you tell those people you tell the  voters who say you've got us into this  mess or you maybe didn't help us get  through the mess the best way well what  I would say is that the mess was one  that wasn't created by any politician  fair enough right so at the end of it  all we had a Health crisis that needed  to be addressed and to help we  determined that our health district was  the principal source of the expertise  and the information and guidance when it  came to the health issues and we worked  very hard to pay attention to what the  Southern Nevada Health District was  advising us we have members from elected  members from the cities in the county  that serve on the board there so we had  whatever minimal input a politician can  can give at the same time we had orders  that were both presidential and  gubernatorial that were affecting  everything travel was not controlled by  us so businesses who wanted to be open  and who even when the time came who  could have reopened had lost the  opportunity because there were no  customers for them it was one of those  times where I'm sure that there were  things that we would do differently  although we're in the process  continuously of trying to figure out  what we might have done differently one  of the big issues really was that we  weren't in control of enough some have  said we should have sued somebody well  I'm a lawyer I've been in court I know  how long it takes to get through we  would still be litigating some of those  issues that wasn't the right thing to do  the right thing to do was press hard  make ourselves a room at the table so  that we are providing the representation  of our businesses and our Industries and  our people in southern Nevada the school  district was was doing it whatever it  felt it needed to do we had no control  over it I don't know who all is saying  it but some have said well we masked  children we didn't mask children we had  no control over what was happening in  the schools but what we did learn was  that the broadband service is inadequate  in this Valley and we have an ongoing  process right now where we're going to  address that we're we're getting very  close  to real solutions we'll talk a little  bit about moving forward in just a  second here sort of post pandemic and if  you're re-elected your next 40 years I  also want to just touch on something  else that your opponent has brought up  and you know you've had a long political  career you were the mayor in Henderson  you've you've been in politics for a  long time here there have been uh  accusations made with regards to ethics  campaign financing debt from when you  are the mayor of Henderson instead of  lingering Financial fiscal issues that  are that are sort of being brought up to  be critical of your performance how do  you tell voters that you're the best  Steward of their money that when you're  in office you're the best person to  represent them and get the county moving  forward well first of all in the city of  Henderson we operated on a really tight  budget and always always were operating  within the parameters that were assigned  so there was no debt you know when there  was a loose comment that wasn't intended  to convey the message that was conveyed  when the mayor who succeeded me said I  inherited a 90 million dollar deficit  well there are no deficit what he  inherited was a circumstance a recession  that was rolling in as I was leaving  office and so we had already done a  voluntary retirement program and we'd  saved considerable money on payroll we  were cutting back in areas that were  really important keeping we had even  debate about do we leave rec centers  open or do we close them no we need  those more than anything they're paid  for people don't have to pay to go there  we we were having those debates as I  left office that the recession deepened  and the challenges uh were difficult we  actually had reserves about 30 million  dollars in reserves  the problem is the the recession was  deep enough that that didn't cover it  all  when it comes to the fiscal  responsibility  I know the budgets I have always been a  guardian of the budget I questioned the  expenditures that we make so that I feel  comfortable that we're going to get a  return on that investment in this  community it's easy to sit back and just  say things and that there's a lot of  that that goes on in any campaign but  the assertion that I have somehow been  unethical and I have been unresponsive  to the financial challenges and  responsibilities that I owe are  ridiculous there is no foundation for  any of that  I can't you know I don't remember all of  the things that you just mentioned but  but the reality is that I think I've  done a good job he even says that I'm a  career politician the reality is my  career is working in the things I've  worked at I had a law firm for many  years today I'm a president of a waste  management Waste Company  I have a day job I have always had a day  job I'm not someone who's relied  exclusively on a pen a a payment from  government to survive I do this because  I want to make a difference and I think  the businesses ably represented by me  not only that I have a large family and  we're everywhere we're in the school  district we're everywhere and I care  about what we leave behind and I've felt  motivated to continue to work in  government because I think I can make a  difference so that the quality of life  that my family enjoys over time when I'm  finished will have been protected so you  know this it's easy to to get up and  make loose uh assertions about people  but let's see what the detail is and I'm  happy to discuss the detail  um I want to talk about the future we  talked a little bit maybe too much about  the past here the future here one of the  major issues for Clark County is water  you and I have talked about this before  if you're re-elected next four years  what can Clark County do you're talking  about getting a seat at the table what  can Clark County do to try and address  what's happening at Lake Mead Beyond  issues with Turf and pools and so forth  well keep in mind that four percent of  the water that comes out of Lake Mead  comes to Nevada four percent so what  what we've been doing is preparing  ourselves for whatever the next  iteration of challenges on the river may  be my opponent thinks that the reason  the lake is going down is that  California is taking too much water out  it wouldn't matter if California were  doing our program  there would still not be enough water in  the lake because there's a drought  when it comes to preparing for the  future keep in mind that the third straw  that we've built a billion and a half  dollars worth of we all paid for we have  no federal money in that  the people who live in southern Nevada  paid for that so no one else can control  what we do with that straws fully  authorized we're the only state  and the only region that will be able to  pump water out of that Lake if it ever  gets to a place where it can't produce  power so in terms of protecting  ourselves there are are 20 or 30 feet of  an entire Lake that are above our intake  and that's a lot of water and we believe  that our 50-year resource plan is a  legitimate plan but there are other  things that are being discussed now  other kinds of things I mean there's a  lot of loose discussion about a building  a canal or building a pipeline and you  know what training Lake Powell well you  can't drain Lake Powell because if the  Western grid goes down what we've  discovered is that the only source of  energy in in an immediate jolt that we  have in the Western United States to  restart the Western grid is Glen Canyon  Dam so that's not an option that's the  reason why that's a reason why the the  department of interior is protecting the  uh the Lake Powell level levels and  they're protecting our levels too so my  feeling is that there will be some  things that may be really different and  cool I don't know exactly where it will  come from but we're looking at every  option but keep in mind we are a very  small state this is a political question  and the more when you start thinking  about what kind of political reach we  have independent lead just by ourselves  hey States let us take some of the water  that is draining and running in your  Rivers that's not an easy sell so we're  we're saying to ourselves we can't rely  on anyone else that's the reason why  we're being pretty aggressive very  aggressive in terms of conserving and  that's the reason we spent a billion and  a half dollars to get something down low  enough that we know will have water for  the for the future final question here  you're re-elected you back in January  2023 first thing you want to do  well the the very first thing we're  going to do is get in front of us the  things that we've been working on and  figure out which of those are going to  be successful and then press forward to  to close the the door uh on things that  make no sense one of the things that  we've done a good job of and I have to  say this  things need to keep going pretty much  the way they're going we're doing a good  job with water we have now figured out  some things that will help us with  affordable housing there's a lot more to  be done but we're on the way when it  comes to so many of the other issues  that are out there the transportation  issues the issues that are relate to  sustainability all of that is something  that we have made enormous progress on  and what we need to do is certify our  energy levels to be where they need to  be and then press forward with the  things that make sense and not we will  continue to do what I said a moment ago  we'll sort out the things that don't  make sense that we can't make progress  on in the immediate future and and we'll  continue to surround ourselves with  people that are smarter than we are and  get the kind of help we need and a  public debate is something that is a  good thing and we'll continue to have it  well thank you for coming in to speak  with us I appreciate it commissioner Jim  Gibson running for re-election in  District G in Clark County good luck  with you thank you

